import React, { createContext, useState, useContext } from 'react';

const TooltipContext = createContext();

const TooltipProvider = ({ children }) => {
  const [tooltip, setTooltip] = useState(null);

  const openTooltip = (tooltip) => {
    setTooltip(tooltip);
  };

  const closeTooltip = () => {
    setTooltip(null);
  };

  return (
    <TooltipContext.Provider value={{ openTooltip, closeTooltip, tooltip }}>
      {children}
    </TooltipContext.Provider>
  );
};

const useTooltip = () => useContext(TooltipContext);

const Tooltip = ({ children, tooltip }) => {
  const { closeTooltip } = useTooltip();

  return (
    <div
      className="tooltip"
      onMouseLeave={() => closeTooltip()}
      style={{
        position: 'absolute',
        top: tooltip.top,
        left: tooltip.left,
        backgroundColor: 'white',
        padding: '10px',
        border: '1px solid black',
      }}
    >
      {children}
    </div>
  );
};

const Button = ({ children, tooltip }) => {
  const { openTooltip, tooltip: currentTooltip } = useTooltip();

  const handleMouseOver = () => {
    openTooltip(tooltip);
  };

  return (
    <div
      onMouseOver={handleMouseOver}
      style={{
        position: 'relative',
        display: 'inline-block',
      }}
    >
      {children}
      {currentTooltip && (
        <Tooltip tooltip={currentTooltip}>
          <p>{currentTooltip.text}</p>
        </Tooltip>
      )}
    </div>
  );
};

const App = () => {
  return (
    <TooltipProvider>
      <Button tooltip={{ top: 20, left: 20, text: 'Hello' }}>
        Hover me
      </Button>
      <Button tooltip={{ top: 40, left: 40, text: 'World' }}>
        Hover me
      </Button>
    </TooltipProvider>
  );
};
```

In this code, we create a `TooltipContext` using the `createContext` function from React. We then create a `TooltipProvider` component that wraps the entire app and provides the `openTooltip`, `closeTooltip`, and `tooltip` state to its children.

We then create a `useTooltip` hook that uses the `useContext` function to access the `TooltipContext` and its values.

The `Tooltip` component uses the `useTooltip` hook to access the `closeTooltip` function and the `tooltip` state. It then renders a div with the tooltip content and closes the tooltip when the mouse leaves the div.

The `Button` component uses the `useTooltip` hook to access the `openTooltip` function and the `tooltip` state. It then renders a div with the button content and opens the tooltip when the mouse hovers over the button. If a tooltip is already open, it renders the tooltip content.

Finally, we create an `App` component that wraps the entire app with the `TooltipProvider` component and renders two `Button` components with different tooltips. When you hover over a button, the corresponding tooltip will be displayed. When you hover over another button, the previous tooltip will be closed and the new tooltip will be displayed. 

